{"answer":"Two separate design choices, both recorded in the job-shape spec (d1/d2):\n\n1. **`small deploy` starts nothing on `kind = \"job\"` apps.** The spec explicitly separates deployment from execution for jobs: deploy builds and registers the image via the control plane, but doesn't launch a machine — a run is a distinct, on-demand event triggered by `small run`, which asks the control plane to start one machine with `auto_destroy: true` for that single execution (d1; packages/cli/bin/small.js:159-166 — 'jobs: build + register the image, start nothing — the control plane starts machines per run'; control-plane apiRunStart at index.js:197-221 starts the Fly machine only when a run is requested).

2. **Why `runner.py` instead of `guard.py`.** Jobs have a fundamentally different runtime shape than servers: no HTTP port, no auth wall, and instead of proxying/guarding requests they need to run an entry script to completion, capture stdout/stderr, and report progress/exit status back to the control plane. `guard.py` is built for the request-serving model (EXPOSE 8080, PORT env); job containers have no port to guard. Instead `runner.py` is a stdlib-only wrapper that runs the entry as a subprocess, batches captured output lines to `/api/runs/<id>/log` with a per-run token, and POSTs the final exit code (d2; packages/cli/lib/generate.js:38-44 — job Dockerfile has 'no port, no guard: runner.py streams output to the control plane and exits'; packages/runtime/runner.py:1-5).

Note: memory size for job machines is still fixed at 256mb rather than read from small.toml — deferred per d5, not part of this question but related to job execution config.","citations":[{"kind":"decision","decision_id":"d1"},{"kind":"decision","decision_id":"d2"},{"kind":"code","path":"packages/cli/bin/small.js","start_line":159,"end_line":166},{"kind":"code","path":"packages/cli/lib/generate.js","start_line":38,"end_line":44},{"kind":"code","path":"packages/runtime/runner.py","start_line":1,"end_line":5},{"kind":"code","path":"packages/control-plane/src/index.js","start_line":197,"end_line":221}]}

{"answer":"The session's debugging trail (m868–m1014) diagnosed this as a silent log-delivery failure, not a job failure. `runner.py`'s log POSTs to `/api/runs/<id>/log` were being blocked by Cloudflare error 1010 — the default `Python-urllib` User-Agent is bot-blocked on workers.dev, so every log POST 403'd (m982). AWS/S3 wasn't affected since AWS doesn't UA-block, so the job itself ran fine and wrote S3 (m902, m807), but zero log lines ever reached the control plane, leaving the run stuck in `status = 'running'` — which is what made the CLI's polling loop in `small run` (packages/cli/bin/small.js:196-206) spin forever waiting for a terminal status.

Compounding this, `runner.py`'s retry/drop logic (packages/runtime/runner.py:23-45) originally swallowed the failure entirely rather than surfacing it, which is why the cause wasn't visible until the agent added a stderr print on the platform console (m907, m923).

The fix: set a custom `User-Agent: small-runner` header on the log POST request (packages/runtime/runner.py:34) to dodge Cloudflare's bot block, plus surfacing dropped-batch failures to the machine's own stderr instead of silently discarding them (packages/runtime/runner.py:43-45). This was verified end-to-end with a rebuilt image showing lines streaming live and the run reaching `finished|0` (m1014), then confirmed via the full integration suite passing (m1033).","citations":[{"kind":"message","message_id":"m982"},{"kind":"message","message_id":"m902"},{"kind":"message","message_id":"m907"},{"kind":"code","path":"packages/runtime/runner.py","start_line":23,"end_line":45},{"kind":"code","path":"packages/cli/bin/small.js","start_line":191,"end_line":207},{"kind":"message","message_id":"m1014"}]}

