Based on this commit's record: no — the spec explicitly ruled out logging query strings and bodies ("Never log request bodies or query strings — paths only"), and guard.py enforces this by stripping the query string before logging (`path.split("?", 1)[0]`, guard.py:37) and never touching the body for the `_log()` call. Rejected requests also deliberately have no user field: they're 403'd *before* `X-Small-User` is even read (guard.py:129-136), so there's no asserted user to log — a rejected request by definition didn't get proxy-authenticated, so the guard has no trustworthy identity to attach. Both docs/v7_request_logs.md (lines 5-8) and guard.py confirm this as intentional design, not an oversight.

My conditional advice: if you want more debug detail, the safer next step is a separate, explicitly-opt-in debug mode (e.g. truncated/hashed query params, never raw bodies) rather than changing default logging — request logs here are meant as a lightweight traffic view, and bodies/query strings can carry PII or secrets. No such debug mode was recorded as planned or deferred in this commit (docs/v7_request_logs.md "Ceilings" section lists only websocket status, retry/spool, retention, search, export — not this).

