{"answer":"Double-fire prevention: on each tick the worker floors `event.scheduledTime` to the minute (`tick`) and only processes an app if `(app.last_scheduled_at || 0) >= tick` is false — i.e. it hasn't already recorded that minute or a later one as fired. As soon as a match is found it immediately writes `UPDATE apps SET last_scheduled_at = tick`, before starting the run, so a duplicate or late-arriving tick for the same minute (or an earlier one) is skipped (index.js:440-452). This is the exact mechanism the spec asked for ('last_scheduled_at on the app so a tick that arrives late doesn't double-fire,' m10).

No, it does not replay missed minutes after downtime. This is an explicit deferred item: the code comment above `scheduled()` states 'ponytail: no catch-up after downtime — a missed minute is just missed' (index.js:438), and the builder's own summary lists 'catch-up runs after downtime' as one of the things explicitly skipped per the original spec's 'Skip:' list (m10, m261, m712). So if the Worker itself is down or a tick is never delivered, that scheduled run is simply lost — `last_scheduled_at` only guards against re-processing a tick that *does* arrive, not against reconstructing ticks that never arrived.

Next step if catch-up behavior is wanted: it would need a new deliberate design (e.g. scanning all missed minutes since `last_scheduled_at` on the next successful tick), which was not built here.","citations":[{"kind":"code","path":"packages/control-plane/src/index.js","start_line":435,"end_line":467},{"kind":"code","path":"packages/control-plane/src/cron.js","start_line":1,"end_line":2},{"kind":"message","message_id":"m10"},{"kind":"message","message_id":"m261"},{"kind":"message","message_id":"m712"}]}

{"answer":"The 4-year cap is a guard against infinite loops on schedules that syntactically parse but never actually occur — the code comment gives the example \"0 0 30 2 *\" (Feb 30th), which is valid cron syntax but no such date exists (code m/cron.js:40 and inline comment, lines 39-41, 44). The loop bound `4 * 366 * 1440` (minutes in 4 leap years) is what enforces this (cron.js:44), and `apiDeploy` in index.js calls `nextRun` at deploy time specifically so this failure surfaces as a 400 error immediately rather than as a silently-dead schedule (index.js:90-95, comment at line 92: \"also rejects '0 0 30 2 *' — valid syntax, never fires\").

There is no recorded validation that four years is an 'optimal performance cutoff' — nothing in the sources or session messages benchmarks loop iteration count, CPU time, or compares alternative bounds. The choice reads as a simple 'long enough to be safe, short enough to fail fast' pick tied to the leap-year edge case the comment calls out, not a tuned performance number. No reasoning beyond that is recorded.

